          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do bodies differ about delayed prescribing for respiratory infections?",
          "answer": "There is guidance for delayed prescribing in respiratory infections. This involves giving a prescription with instructions to fill it only if symptoms do not improve or worsen by a certain time. The aim is to avoid unnecessary antibiotic use while allowing patients access to treatment if needed. This concept is part of broader efforts in antibiotic stewardship.\n\nDifferent organizations may have varying recommendations on delayed prescriptions for respiratory infections. Some may support this approach, while others emphasize immediate antibiotics based on symptom duration or severity. The key point is that the guidance surrounding antibiotic use is influenced by the specific infection and patient circumstances.\n\nI could not find specific differences among various bodies regarding delayed prescribing for respiratory infections; if you need detailed comparisons on specific organizations or additional aspects, please let me know!\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
